---

The two phenomena detailed below, syncope and assimilation, exist in Icelandic to make the words easier to pronounce. The exact rules by which these phenomena occur are not important, but it is important that you can recognize them happening. 

## Syncope
In Nordic languages, including Icelandic, syncope refers to a phenomenon where one or more sounds or syllables are omitted from a word, often to simplify pronunciation. As a general rule, nordic languages don't like for words to contain more than two syllables unless they are compounds, so when words become three syllables because of endings being added, they will often ommit non-stress syllables to reduce teh word be two syllables. 

Example: Gamalar -> Gamlar

In the lesson Adjectives, more examples of this sort will be provided.  

## Assimilation
Assimilation is the changing of consonants to be more similar to each other to simplify pronunciation. 

Example: Hraðt -> Hratt

The assimilation above happend because *-ðt* would be a very hard consonant cluster to pronounce, so the ending is changed to *-tt** to make it easier. 

